---
import CodeBlock from "../../components/CodeBlock.astro";
import DocsLayout from "../../layouts/DocsLayout.astro";

const jsonSchema = `{
  "$schema": "./schema.json",
  "name": "example"
}`;
const jsoncSchema = `{
  // JSONC and JSON5 use the same root property
  "$schema": "./schema.json",
  "name": "example",
}`;
const yamlDirective = `# yaml-language-server: $schema=./schema.json
name: example`;
const yamlRoot = `$schema: ./schema.json
name: example`;
const tomlDirective = `#:schema ./schema.json
name = "example"`;
const tomlRoot = `"$schema" = "./schema.json"
name = "example"`;
const association = `[[schemas.associations]]
file = "*.jsonl"
schema = "./schemas/event.schema.json"`;
---

<DocsLayout title="Schema declarations" current="/guides/schema-declarations/">
  <article class="doc">
    <header class="doc-header">
      <span class="eyebrow">Guide</span>
      <h1>Schema declarations</h1>
      <p class="lead">
        Every file can declare its own schema. For files that can't (or
        shouldn't) carry a <code>$schema</code>, config-level associations have
        you covered.
      </p>
    </header>

    <h2>JSON family</h2>
    <CodeBlock code={jsonSchema} language="json" />
    <CodeBlock code={jsoncSchema} language="json" />
    <p>
      JSON, JSONC, and JSON5 use the conventional root <code>$schema</code>
      property. JSON Lines files are validated one non-empty line at a time, so cover
      <code>.jsonl</code> and <code>.ndjson</code> files with a config association,
      CLI association, or catalog match.
    </p>

    <h2>YAML</h2>
    <CodeBlock code={yamlDirective} language="yaml" />
    <CodeBlock code={yamlRoot} language="yaml" />
    <p>
      The comment directive follows the YAML Language Server convention.
      DollarLint scans the first 25 lines for it before looking for a root <code
        >$schema</code
      > key.
    </p>

    <h2>TOML</h2>
    <CodeBlock code={tomlDirective} language="toml" />
    <CodeBlock code={tomlRoot} language="toml" />
    <p>
      The comment directive follows the Taplo and Even Better TOML convention. A
      root <code>$schema</code> key also works, but it must be quoted because <code
        >$</code
      > is not a bare TOML key character.
    </p>

    <h2>Config associations</h2>
    <p>
      When you can't — or don't want to — add a declaration to each file,
      associations let you cover them from your config instead.
    </p>
    <CodeBlock code={association} language="toml" />
    <p>
      Association globs are matched against each discovered file's path relative
      to the validation root. Like discovery globs, a pattern without a slash
      can match a basename at any depth.
    </p>

    <div class="note">
      <p>
        Precedence order: in-file declaration first, then config association,
        then DollarLint's built-in <code>.dollarlint.toml</code> association, then
        catalog match. If none of those hit, the file gets skipped unless <code
          >schemas.requireCoverage = true</code
        >.
      </p>
    </div>
  </article>
</DocsLayout>
